        "original_id": "73749835-3e42-564e-9de4-2325c9457f72",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Events: General Survey Act, Gibbons v. Ogden\n- Organizations: Supreme Court of the United States, Congress\n- Concepts: interstate commerce, federal authority, navigation, Commerce Clause\n\nFacts:\n- In 1824, Gibbons v. Ogden, 22 U.S. 1, became a landmark Supreme Court of the United States decision.\n- In Gibbons v. Ogden, the Supreme Court of the United States held that Congress's Commerce Clause power to regulate interstate commerce includes the power to regulate navigation.\n- The General Survey Act was passed shortly after Gibbons v. Ogden.\n- The General Survey Act followed years of discussion showing that national-scope infrastructure was needed.\n- The passage of the General Survey Act closely followed Gibbons v. Ogden, which first established federal authority over interstate commerce, including river navigation.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "d33ec760-cc05-55d6-ab5c-c2d8e202b433",
        "text": "Page: General Survey Act. Sentence: While such infrastructure of national scope had been discussed and shown wanting for years, its passage shortly followed the landmark Supreme Court ruling, Gibbons v. Ogden, which first established federal authority over interstate commerce including navigation by river.\nPage: Gibbons v. Ogden. Sentence: Gibbons v. Ogden, 22 U.S. 1 (1824), was a landmark decision in which the Supreme Court of the United States held that the power to regulate interstate commerce, granted to Congress by the Commerce Clause of the United States Constitution, encompassed the power to regulate navigation.",
